WEBVTT

00:00:03.430 --> 00:00:05.470
 MemodoAI just got an update.

00:00:05.470 --> 00:00:07.870
 In the next few minutes, we'll cover what's new.

00:00:07.870 --> 00:00:12.600
 The headline being that MemodoAI can now work with your Microsoft 365.

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:18.180
 Plus, reusable skills, chat projects, and a handful of quality of life touches.

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:22.630
 Everything you already know still works exactly the same.

00:00:22.630 --> 00:00:32.340
 The biggest addition, MemodoAI can now read your own Microsoft 365.

00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:39.470
 Your Outlook mail, calendar, OneDrive, and SharePoint files, Excel, OneNote, tasks,

00:00:39.470 --> 00:00:41.920
 and contacts, right inside a chat.

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:45.410
 It works through what's called an MCP server.

00:00:45.410 --> 00:00:49.600
 Think of it as a connector that lets the assistant reach an outside system on your

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:50.180
 behalf.

00:00:50.180 --> 00:00:58.490
 You turn it on in the Composer, click MCP Servers, and tick Microsoft 365 Aligned.

00:00:58.490 --> 00:01:00.120
 A quick note on the connection.

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:04.580
 If you see a green dot next to Microsoft 365, it's live.

00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:08.200
 If it's disconnected, which can happen when your session ages out,

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:12.160
 click the small plug icon and then Tune Initialize.

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:16.500
 A couple of seconds later, it flips to Active, and you're good.

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:18.580
 Now, I'll ask something simple.

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:23.390
 Using Microsoft 365, what is my display name and job title?

00:01:23.390 --> 00:01:25.810
 Notice the line under the response.

00:01:25.810 --> 00:01:29.110
 Ran get current user in Microsoft 365.

00:01:29.110 --> 00:01:32.070
 That tells you exactly which tool it used.

00:01:32.070 --> 00:01:33.530
 You can click it to see more.

00:01:33.530 --> 00:01:35.320
 Three things to keep in mind.

00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:37.280
 First, it runs as you.

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:40.460
 It uses your own Microsoft sign-in,

00:01:40.460 --> 00:01:43.200
 so it can only ever see what you could already see.

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:45.750
 Second, it's read-only for now.

00:01:45.750 --> 00:01:47.860
 It can read your mail and files,

00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:50.580
 but it won't send, edit, or delete anything.

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:52.850
 And third, name a target.

00:01:52.850 --> 00:01:57.580
 Find the ACME contract in SharePoint

00:01:57.580 --> 00:02:00.480
 works far better than Find My Important Documents.

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:03.400
 Anything it reads back still passes through

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:06.170
 the same personal data checks as the rest of Momodo AI.

00:02:08.680 --> 00:02:12.520
 The second big addition is skills.

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:15.020
 A skill is a small, reusable playbook,

00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:18.390
 a set of instructions for how you handle a recurring task

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:21.470
 that the assistant pulls in only when it's relevant,

00:02:21.470 --> 00:02:24.850
 instead of you retyping the same guidance every time.

00:02:24.850 --> 00:02:27.590
 The clever part is that Momodo AI always knows

00:02:27.590 --> 00:02:30.080
 a skill's name and one-line description,

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:32.320
 but only loads the full instructions

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:34.670
 when a task actually matches,

00:02:34.670 --> 00:02:37.600
 so the assistant can effectively know

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:39.600
 a whole library of procedures

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:42.830
 without carrying all of them in every conversation.

00:02:42.830 --> 00:02:45.410
 There are two ways to use one.

00:02:45.410 --> 00:02:47.860
 You can just let the assistant pick it.

00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:50.380
 An enabled skill applies automatically

00:02:50.380 --> 00:02:52.930
 when your request matches its description,

00:02:52.930 --> 00:02:55.150
 or you can invoke it by name.

00:02:55.150 --> 00:02:57.620
 Type a dollar sign in the message box

00:02:57.620 --> 00:02:59.940
 and a picker lists your skills.

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:01.750
 I'll pick meeting notes

00:03:01.750 --> 00:03:05.230
 and paste in a rough team sync transcript.

00:03:05.230 --> 00:03:06.510
 Look at the result.

00:03:06.510 --> 00:03:08.450
 The assistant reshaped my messy notes

00:03:08.450 --> 00:03:13.990
 into clean sections, attendees, decisions, action items with owners and due dates,

00:03:13.990 --> 00:03:14.410
 and open

00:03:14.410 --> 00:03:20.190
 questions. That structure came from the skill, not from me asking for it. You

00:03:20.190 --> 00:03:22.520
 manage skills from the

00:03:22.520 --> 00:03:27.790
 skills panel in the left rail. That's where you create, edit, and organize them. To

00:03:27.790 --> 00:03:28.480
 make one,

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:35.160
 click the plus and choose write skill instructions. Give it a name, a description

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:36.110
 that's specific

00:03:36.110 --> 00:03:40.960
 about when it should apply, and the instructions themselves in Markdown. A pattern

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:42.100
 that works well,

00:03:42.100 --> 00:03:48.540
 borrowed from the skills already in here, is three headings. When to use, procedure,

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:55.790
 and output format. Save it, and it's in your library. One more thing worth knowing.

00:03:55.790 --> 00:03:56.660
 Skills

00:03:56.660 --> 00:04:01.510
 are versioned and can be shared, so a team can build up a trusted set of playbooks.

00:04:01.510 --> 00:04:03.310
 Fix a procedure

00:04:03.310 --> 00:04:11.650
 once and everyone using it gets the fix. Two more capabilities came with this

00:04:11.650 --> 00:04:13.330
 update. The first is

00:04:13.330 --> 00:04:18.950
 for people who build agents, chaining. In the agent builder under advanced, there's

00:04:18.950 --> 00:04:19.940
 a multi-agent

00:04:19.940 --> 00:04:24.170
 orchestration section with a chain option. It lets you run a fixed sequence of

00:04:24.170 --> 00:04:25.750
 agents one after

00:04:25.750 --> 00:04:32.480
 another. The output of one feeds the next. Here you can see a chain where a primary

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:33.380
 agent hands

00:04:33.380 --> 00:04:38.860
 off to an uppercase bot that transforms its output. It's worth reaching for when a

00:04:38.860 --> 00:04:39.120
 task

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:45.190
 has genuinely distinct steps. Just remember each step adds tokens and time, so most

00:04:45.190 --> 00:04:46.460
 everyday tasks

00:04:46.460 --> 00:04:53.130
 don't need it. The second is memory, and this one's for everyone. Memido AI can now

00:04:53.130 --> 00:04:53.600
 actively

00:04:53.600 --> 00:05:00.340
 remember things you tell it. Watch. I'll say, from now on, remember that I'm on the

00:05:00.340 --> 00:05:01.430
 front-end team

00:05:01.430 --> 00:05:04.350
 and I prefer concise, bulleted answers.

00:05:04.350 --> 00:05:07.200
 The Assistant confirms what it saved

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:08.620
 right there in its reply.

00:05:08.620 --> 00:05:11.530
 Those items now live in your Memories panel,

00:05:11.530 --> 00:05:13.430
 the brain icon in the left rail,

00:05:13.430 --> 00:05:16.390
 where you can read, edit, or delete them.

00:05:16.390 --> 00:05:18.810
 It's tied to your account only

00:05:18.810 --> 00:05:21.250
 and there's a toggle to turn it off whenever you want.

00:05:21.250 --> 00:05:22.940
 There's nothing to set up.

00:05:22.940 --> 00:05:25.740
 It just works wherever you chat.

00:05:25.740 --> 00:05:32.630
 If your chat history is getting long,

00:05:32.630 --> 00:05:34.040
 projects will help.

00:05:34.040 --> 00:05:36.520
 You can now group related conversations

00:05:36.520 --> 00:05:39.820
 into project folders shown at the top of the sidebar.

00:05:39.820 --> 00:05:42.560
 Click New Project, give it a name,

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:46.500
 and you've got a home for, say, all your Q3 planning chats.

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:50.210
 Think of projects as folders that group conversations,

00:05:50.210 --> 00:05:52.970
 where bookmarks, which you already have,

00:05:52.970 --> 00:05:55.410
 are tags you apply across them.

00:05:55.410 --> 00:06:02.720
 A few smaller touches you'll notice as you work.

00:06:02.720 --> 00:06:05.090
 There's a new context meter,

00:06:05.090 --> 00:06:07.270
 the little circle in the message bar.

00:06:07.270 --> 00:06:09.920
 Hover it, and you'll see how full

00:06:09.920 --> 00:06:16.560
 the model's context window is for the current chat. It's a usage gauge, not a bill.

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:17.580
 It shows

00:06:17.580 --> 00:06:22.750
 how much room is left, not a cost. When a long chat starts to fill up, that's a

00:06:22.750 --> 00:06:23.630
 good moment to

00:06:23.630 --> 00:06:29.210
 start a fresh one. Office files look better too. When you attach a Word doc, a

00:06:29.210 --> 00:06:29.880
 spreadsheet,

00:06:29.880 --> 00:06:35.350
 a slide deck, or a CSV, it now shows as a proper preview card rather than a plain

00:06:35.350 --> 00:06:36.700
 download link.

00:06:36.700 --> 00:06:41.760
 And a couple of little things. New conversations get a title immediately instead of

00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:42.170
 after the

00:06:42.170 --> 00:06:48.170
 first reply, and hovering any message shows you when it was sent. That's what's new.

00:06:48.170 --> 00:06:49.180
 If you want

00:06:49.180 --> 00:06:54.210
 a refresher on the basics, the model picker, file search, building agents, the main

00:06:54.210 --> 00:06:55.140
 getting started

00:06:55.140 --> 00:07:00.310
 guide covers all of that, and none of it changed. Thanks for watching.

